{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "An object is dropped from rest. After falling distance x, its momentum is p. What is its momentum after falling 2x? A) p  B) √2p  C) p/2  D) 2p"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is lost, so begin with the basic relationship between momentum and velocity.",
      "content": "Let’s take it one step at a time. What equation connects momentum, mass and velocity?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think p = mv? But I don't see what falling distance changes."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Confirm the relationship, then guide the learner to connect fall distance with speed using the equation for an object dropped from rest.",
      "content": "That’s right: p = mv. For an object dropped from rest, its speed after falling distance d follows v² = 2gd. What is v² after it falls distance x?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "2gx?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly substitutes x. At x, v = √(2gx); at 2x, v′ = √(4gx). The speed ratio is √(4gx)/√(2gx) = √(4/2) = √2. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"sqrt(4*g*x)/sqrt(2*g*x)\") -> sqrt(2)]. Since mass is unchanged, momentum has the same ratio to its original value.",
      "content": "Yes! At 2x, the speed is √(4gx), which is √2 times the speed at x. The mass stays the same, so the momentum is also multiplied by √2. The answer is **B) √2p**."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "B",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nratio = sp.simplify(sp.sqrt(4)/sp.sqrt(2))\nprint(\"B\" if ratio == sp.sqrt(2) else \"check\")",
    "techniques": [
      "Use v² = u² + 2aΔx for an object dropped from rest",
      "Compare speeds using a ratio",
      "Apply p = mv for constant mass"
    ]
  }
}

